    {id="auto-theme-helping-others-is-a-kind-choice-fad032", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="theme", priority=5, prompt="Omar notices a classmate dropped crayons, so he stops to help pick them up. Which theme fits?", answer="Helping others is a kind choice.", hint="Think about the message shown by Omar’s action.", explanation="Omar chooses to help someone who needs it.", choices={"Crayons are difficult to carry.","Classrooms should have no colors.","Helping others is a kind choice."}},
    {id="auto-theme-trying-something-difficult-can-build-confidence-3c5ddf", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="theme", priority=5, prompt="Kai is nervous to read aloud but tries anyway and feels proud afterward. Which theme fits?", answer="Trying something difficult can build confidence.", hint="Choose a lesson that could apply beyond this story.", explanation="Kai acts despite being nervous and gains confidence.", choices={"Trying something difficult can build confidence.","Reading should only happen silently.","Feeling nervous means you should always stop."}},
    {id="auto-theme-it-is-a-broad-lesson-that-can-apply-to-many-situations-e317f9", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="theme", priority=5, prompt="Why is “Friends can solve problems by working together” a theme instead of a story detail?", answer="It is a broad lesson that can apply to many situations.", hint="Ask whether the idea could apply to another story.", explanation="Themes are broader messages, not single facts.", choices={"It tells exactly where the story happens.","It is a broad lesson that can apply to many situations.","It names one object in the story."}},
    {id="auto-theme-taking-responsibility-helps-repair-mistakes-7f93b2", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="theme", priority=5, prompt="A character admits a mistake and fixes what was damaged. Which message is the strongest theme?", answer="Taking responsibility helps repair mistakes.", hint="Focus on the lesson from the character’s choice.", explanation="The character owns the mistake and works to make it right.", choices={"Damaged things are always useless.","Mistakes should always be hidden.","Taking responsibility helps repair mistakes."}},
    {id="auto-visualize-a-gray-rainy-scene-outside-a-window-525c8d", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="visualize", priority=5, prompt="Read: “Tiny raindrops tapped the window while gray clouds covered the sky.” Which picture best matches?", answer="A gray rainy scene outside a window", hint="Match the picture to the exact describing words.", explanation="The words raindrops, window, and gray clouds create that mental picture.", choices={"A gray rainy scene outside a window","A bright beach with large waves","A sunny playground with no clouds"}},
    {id="auto-visualize-the-path-is-covered-with-golden-leaves-431aeb", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="visualize", priority=5, prompt="Which detail is most important for visualizing “golden leaves covered the path like a crunchy blanket”?", answer="The path is covered with golden leaves.", hint="Choose a detail you could actually picture in your mind.", explanation="Color and where the leaves are located are concrete details that build the mental picture.", choices={"Someone may walk later.","The path is covered with golden leaves.","The sentence has many words."}},
    {id="auto-visualize-a-red-kite-moving-in-a-windy-blue-sky-19c580", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="visualize", priority=5, prompt="Read: “A bright red kite dipped and climbed in the windy blue sky.” What should you picture?", answer="A red kite moving in a windy blue sky", hint="Use only details the sentence provides.", explanation="The sentence gives color, movement, wind, and sky details.", choices={"A dark cave with a lantern","A snowy road at night","A red kite moving in a windy blue sky"}},
    {id="auto-visualize-warm-soup-by-a-rainy-window-857cd6", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="visualize", priority=5, prompt="Read: “Steam curled above the warm soup while rain tapped the kitchen window.” Which picture matches?", answer="Warm soup by a rainy window", hint="Match several details at once.", explanation="Steam, soup, rain, and the window create that mental picture.", choices={"Warm soup by a rainy window","A dry sandbox in bright sun","A frozen lake under stars"}},
    {id="auto-visualize-a-small-puppy-resting-in-a-blue-blanket-8b2f53", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="visualize", priority=5, prompt="Read: “The tiny puppy curled into the soft blue blanket beside the couch.” What should you visualize?", answer="A small puppy resting in a blue blanket", hint="Picture the specific nouns and describing words.", explanation="The details describe the puppy, blanket, and location.", choices={"A horse running across a field","A small puppy resting in a blue blanket","A bird sitting on a roof"}},
    {id="auto-visualize-silver-shimmered-quiet-pond-e89578", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="visualize", priority=5, prompt="Which words do the most work in helping you visualize “silver moonlight shimmered across the quiet pond”?", answer="silver, shimmered, quiet pond", hint="Choose words that create a sensory picture.", explanation="Those concrete describing words give color, movement, and setting.", choices={"the, across, the","moonlight is a word","silver, shimmered, quiet pond"}},
    {id="auto-visualize-it-gives-specific-sensory-and-location-details-488b64", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="visualize", priority=5, prompt="Why does “crunchy brown leaves covered the sidewalk” create a clearer picture than “things were outside”?", answer="It gives specific sensory and location details.", hint="Compare how much you can actually picture.", explanation="Specific details make the mental image clearer.", choices={"It gives specific sensory and location details.","It has more letters.","It uses the word sidewalk."}},
    {id="auto-dialogue-i-found-my-book-1db336", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="dialogue", priority=5, prompt="Which part of this sentence is dialogue: “I found my book!” Lena shouted.", answer="“I found my book!”", hint="Look for the words the character actually says.", explanation="Dialogue is the exact speech of a character and is shown inside quotation marks.", choices={"Lena shouted.","“I found my book!”","my book"}},
    {id="auto-dialogue-they-show-the-exact-words-a-character-says-6fe19d", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="dialogue", priority=5, prompt="Why do quotation marks help a reader recognize dialogue?", answer="They show the exact words a character says.", hint="Think about what information quotation marks surround.", explanation="Quotation marks separate a character’s spoken words from the narration around them.", choices={"They show where the story takes place.","They tell how many paragraphs are in a story.","They show the exact words a character says."}},
    {id="auto-dialogue-i-can-help-b8321e", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="dialogue", priority=5, prompt="Which words are dialogue in: “I can help,” Noah said.", answer="“I can help,”", hint="Find the words the character actually says.", explanation="The quotation marks show Noah’s exact spoken words.", choices={"“I can help,”","Noah said.","help Noah"}},
    {id="auto-dialogue-the-baby-is-sleeping-3a44a5", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="dialogue", priority=5, prompt="Read: Ava whispered, “The baby is sleeping.” What did Ava say?", answer="“The baby is sleeping.”", hint="Look inside the quotation marks.", explanation="The words inside quotation marks are Ava’s dialogue.", choices={"Ava whispered.","“The baby is sleeping.”","The narrator said Ava was loud."}},
    {id="auto-dialogue-let-s-go-outside-ben-said-dca232", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="dialogue", priority=5, prompt="Which sentence uses dialogue correctly?", answer="“Let’s go outside,” Ben said.", hint="Find the sentence that clearly marks spoken words.", explanation="The character’s exact words are placed inside quotation marks.", choices={"Let’s go outside, Ben said.","Ben said lets “go outside”.","“Let’s go outside,” Ben said."}},
    {id="auto-dialogue-those-are-mia-s-exact-spoken-words-01300a", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="dialogue", priority=5, prompt="In “Wait for me!” Mia called, what do the quotation marks tell the reader?", answer="Those are Mia’s exact spoken words.", hint="Think about what quotation marks surround.", explanation="Quotation marks separate dialogue from narration.", choices={"Those are Mia’s exact spoken words.","Mia is the narrator.","The story happens outside."}},
    {id="auto-dialogue-the-quoted-words-are-spoken-dad-said-tells-who-spoke-813305", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="dialogue", priority=5, prompt="Why is “Dad said, ‘Dinner is ready.’” partly dialogue and partly narration?", answer="The quoted words are spoken; “Dad said” tells who spoke.", hint="Separate the quoted words from the speech tag.", explanation="Dialogue gives exact speech while narration identifies the speaker.", choices={"Every word is dialogue.","The quoted words are spoken; “Dad said” tells who spoke.","None of the words are spoken."}},
    {id="auto-main-character-ben-34a10c", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="main-character", priority=5, prompt="A story follows Ben as he trains for a race, worries before the start, and celebrates at the finish. Who is the main character?", answer="Ben", hint="Choose the person the story follows most closely.", explanation="The story follows Ben’s actions and feelings across the whole sequence.", choices={"the race","the finish line","Ben"}},
    {id="auto-main-character-most-of-the-important-events-happen-to-or-because-of-ben-61094e", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="main-character", priority=5, prompt="Why is Ben the main character in a story that follows his problem, choices, and result?", answer="Most of the important events happen to or because of Ben.", hint="Think about whose actions drive the story.", explanation="The main character is central to the important events and changes in the story.", choices={"Most of the important events happen to or because of Ben.","His name has three letters.","He appears only in the title."}},
    {id="auto-subject-predicate-sing-in-the-tree-741dd3", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="subject-predicate", priority=5, prompt="In the sentence “The birds sing in the tree,” which words are the predicate?", answer="sing in the tree", hint="First find the subject, then choose the words that tell what it does.", explanation="The predicate tells what the subject does: the birds sing in the tree.", choices={"The birds","sing in the tree","the tree"}},
    {id="auto-subject-predicate-mia-is-the-subject-opened-the-window-is-the-predicate-28f606", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="subject-predicate", priority=5, prompt="Which explanation correctly separates the subject and predicate in “Mia opened the window”?", answer="Mia is the subject; opened the window is the predicate.", hint="The subject names who or what; the predicate tells what that subject does or is.", explanation="Mia names who the sentence is about, and opened the window tells what Mia did.", choices={"Opened is the subject; Mia is the predicate.","The window is the subject; Mia opened is the predicate.","Mia is the subject; opened the window is the predicate."}},
    {id="auto-subject-predicate-my-little-brother-2af298", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="subject-predicate", priority=5, prompt="In “My little brother builds a tower,” which words are the subject?", answer="My little brother", hint="Ask who is doing the action.", explanation="The subject tells who the sentence is about.", choices={"My little brother","builds a tower","a tower"}},
    {id="auto-subject-predicate-stopped-by-the-school-d4895a", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="subject-predicate", priority=5, prompt="In “The yellow bus stopped by the school,” which words are the predicate?", answer="stopped by the school", hint="Find the action and the words that go with it.", explanation="The predicate tells what the yellow bus did.", choices={"The yellow bus","stopped by the school","the school"}},
    {id="auto-subject-predicate-subject-the-rabbit-predicate-hopped-across-the-yard-18d487", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="subject-predicate", priority=5, prompt="Which pair correctly labels the parts of “The rabbit hopped across the yard”?", answer="subject: The rabbit; predicate: hopped across the yard", hint="Split the sentence into who or what, then what that subject does.", explanation="The rabbit names who the sentence is about; hopped across the yard tells what it did.", choices={"subject: hopped; predicate: The rabbit","subject: the yard; predicate: The rabbit hopped","subject: The rabbit; predicate: hopped across the yard"}},
    {id="auto-subject-predicate-it-names-what-the-sentence-is-about-d28466", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="subject-predicate", priority=5, prompt="Why is “The tall tree” the subject in “The tall tree swayed in the wind”?", answer="It names what the sentence is about.", hint="Decide whether the words name the subject or tell what it does.", explanation="A subject names who or what the sentence is about.", choices={"It names what the sentence is about.","It tells what the tree did.","It names where the action happened."}},
    {id="auto-subject-predicate-our-class-read-a-new-book-df7483", subject="Reading / ELA", skill="subject-predicate", priority=5, prompt="Which sentence is correctly split into subject and predicate?", answer="Our class | read a new book.", hint="Keep all the subject words together, then all the words telling what the subject does.", explanation="Our class is the complete subject, and read a new book is the predicate.", choices={"Read a | new book our class.","Our class | read a new book.","A new book | our class read."}},
    {id="auto-short-i-long-i-five-4be63d", subject="Spelling / Handwriting", skill="short-i-long-i", priority=5, prompt="Which word has the long i sound you hear in 'kite'?", answer="five", hint="Listen for the vowel that sounds like the name of the letter i.", explanation="Five has long i, like kite. Milk and spin have short i, as in sit.", choices={"milk","spin","five"}},
    {id="auto-short-i-long-i-ship-and-grin-d66822", subject="Spelling / Handwriting", skill="short-i-long-i", priority=5, prompt="Which pair has a short i sound in both words?", answer="ship and grin", hint="Check both words in each pair. Listen for the vowel sound you hear in 'fit'.", explanation="Ship and grin both have short i. Line and lip have different i sounds. Ride and pine both have long i.", choices={"ship and grin","line and lip","ride and pine"}},
    {id="auto-short-i-long-i-it-changes-from-short-i-to-long-i-381658", subject="Spelling / Handwriting", skill="short-i-long-i", priority=5, prompt="You turn 'kit' into 'kite' by adding e. How does the i sound change?", answer="It changes from short i to long i.", hint="Say the first word and the new word. Listen to what happens in the middle.", explanation="Kit has short i. In kite, the final e is silent and helps signal the long i sound.", choices={"It stays short i.","It changes from short i to long i.","It changes from long i to short i."}},
    {id="auto-short-i-long-i-mice-f1f668", subject="Spelling / Handwriting", skill="short-i-long-i", priority=5, prompt="Which word rhymes with 'rice' and has the same long i sound?", answer="mice", hint="Say the example and each choice. Compare the vowel and the sounds that follow it.", explanation="Mice and rice have the same long i sound and the same ending sound. Race has long a. Miss has short i.", choices={"race","miss","mice"}},
    {id="auto-short-i-long-i-slim-d5a1ff", subject="Spelling / Handwriting", skill="short-i-long-i", priority=5, prompt="Read: 'The slim cat ran home.' Which word has the short i sound?", answer="slim", hint="Read the choices slowly. Listen to the middle of each word.", explanation="Slim has short i, like sit. Cat has short a, and home has long o.", choices={"slim","cat","home"}},
    {id="auto-short-i-long-i-replace-slide-with-swim-e2140e", subject="Spelling / Handwriting", skill="short-i-long-i", priority=5, prompt="The short i group is 'lip, grin, slide'. Which change makes every word in the group have short i?", answer="Replace slide with swim.", hint="Say each word, then check how each suggested swap changes the vowel sounds.", explanation="Lip and grin already have short i. Slide has long i. Replacing it with swim gives three short i words.", choices={"Replace lip with time.","Replace slide with swim.","Replace grin with bike."}},
    {id="auto-short-i-long-i-sit-and-time-bb5d66", subject="Spelling / Handwriting", skill="short-i-long-i", priority=5, prompt="A child says every word with the letter i has the same vowel sound. Which pair shows that the sounds can be different?", answer="sit and time", hint="Read both words in each pair. Check whether their vowel sounds match.", explanation="Sit has short i, but time has long i. Bike and five both have long i. Fish and milk both have short i.", choices={"bike and five","fish and milk","sit and time"}},
    {id="auto-short-i-long-i-smile-and-bike-have-long-i-swim-has-short-i-01b042", subject="Spelling / Handwriting", skill="short-i-long-i", priority=5, prompt="A child groups 'smile' with 'bike' instead of 'swim'. Which reason matches the vowel sounds?", answer="Smile and bike have long i; swim has short i.", hint="Read the three words aloud. Group them by the sound of i, rather than their first letters.", explanation="Smile and bike have long i, the sound of the letter name. Swim has short i, as in fit. Words can share a first letter but have different vowel sounds.", choices={"Smile and bike have long i; swim has short i.","Words that start with s always have short i.","All three words use the same vowel sound."}},
    {id="auto-math-subtraction-9-aefa5b", subject="Math", skill="math-subtraction", priority=4, prompt="Solve 15 - 6. What is the difference?", answer="9", hint="Count back 6 from 15.", explanation="15 take away 6 leaves 9.", choices={"8","9","10"}},
    {id="auto-math-subtraction-6-0267c4", subject="Math", skill="math-subtraction", priority=4, prompt="Solve 10 - 4. What is the difference?", answer="6", hint="Start at 10 and count back 4.", explanation="10 take away 4 leaves 6.", choices={"5","7","6"}},
    {id="auto-math-subtraction-9-0f1872", subject="Math", skill="math-subtraction", priority=4, prompt="Mia has 14 stickers and gives 5 away. How many stickers does she have left?", answer="9", hint="Start with 14 and take away 5.", explanation="14 - 5 = 9, so Mia has 9 stickers left.", choices={"9","8","10"}},
    {id="auto-math-subtraction-9-f11516", subject="Math", skill="math-subtraction", priority=4, prompt="There are 13 birds on a fence. Four fly away. How many birds remain?", answer="9", hint="Take 4 away from 13.", explanation="13 - 4 = 9, so 9 birds remain.", choices={"8","9","10"}},
    {id="auto-math-subtraction-9-5b2cb6", subject="Math", skill="math-subtraction", priority=4, prompt="A box has 16 blocks. Seven blocks are put away. How many blocks are still in the box?", answer="9", hint="Subtract the 7 blocks that were put away from 16.", explanation="16 - 7 = 9, so 9 blocks are still in the box.", choices={"8","10","9"}},
    {id="auto-math-subtraction-8-3-11-b6c78e", subject="Math", skill="math-subtraction", priority=4, prompt="Which addition fact correctly checks 11 - 3 = 8?", answer="8 + 3 = 11", hint="Use the same three numbers to build a related addition fact.", explanation="The difference plus the amount taken away should equal the starting number.", choices={"8 + 3 = 11","11 + 3 = 14","8 + 11 = 19"}},
    {id="auto-math-subtraction-5-4-9-3c0d03", subject="Math", skill="math-subtraction", priority=4, prompt="Sam says 9 - 4 = 6. Which fact shows that his answer is one too high?", answer="5 + 4 = 9", hint="Find the number that plus 4 makes 9.", explanation="The related addition fact 5 + 4 = 9 shows that 9 - 4 = 5.", choices={"6 + 4 = 10","5 + 4 = 9","9 + 4 = 13"}},
    {id="auto-math-subtraction-eight-blocks-are-on-a-table-three-are-put-away-and-five-remain-650883", subject="Math", skill="math-subtraction", priority=4, prompt="Which story problem matches the subtraction fact 8 - 3?", answer="Eight blocks are on a table, three are put away, and five remain.", hint="Look for a story that starts with 8 and takes 3 away.", explanation="The story starts with 8 and removes 3, which matches 8 - 3.", choices={"Three blocks are on a table and eight more are added.","Eight blocks are split into eight equal groups.","Eight blocks are on a table, three are put away, and five remain."}},
    {id="auto-religion-trinity-the-trinity-7cc1ac", subject="Religion", skill="religion-trinity", priority=2, prompt="According to the current Religion lesson, a note says “one God in three Persons: Father, Son, and Holy Spirit.” Which lesson topic does that statement describe?", answer="the Trinity", hint="Match the three Persons named in the statement to the lesson topic.", explanation="The current lesson uses one God in three Persons — Father, Son, and Holy Spirit — to explain the Trinity.", choices={"the Trinity","being made in God's image and likeness","caring for God's creation"}},
    {id="auto-religion-trinity-one-god-in-three-persons-father-son-and-holy-spirit-a3e37c", subject="Religion", skill="religion-trinity", priority=2, prompt="According to the current Religion lesson, which statement best explains the Trinity?", answer="One God in three Persons: Father, Son, and Holy Spirit.", hint="Keep both parts of the lesson together: one God and three Persons.", explanation="The current lesson describes the Trinity as one God in three Persons.", choices={"People are made in God’s image and likeness and can think, choose, and love.","One God in three Persons: Father, Son, and Holy Spirit.","We are responsible for caring for God’s gift of creation."}},
    {id="auto-religion-image-likeness-being-made-in-god-s-image-and-likeness-480aa4", subject="Religion", skill="religion-image-likeness", priority=2, prompt="According to the current Religion lesson, which lesson idea is explained by the words “we can think, choose, and love”?", answer="being made in God's image and likeness", hint="Match the three abilities to the lesson concept they explain.", explanation="The current lesson connects thinking, choosing, and loving with being made in God's image and likeness.", choices={"the Trinity","using our senses to enjoy creation","being made in God's image and likeness"}},
    {id="auto-religion-image-likeness-god-s-image-and-likeness-people-can-think-choose-and-love-2af68f", subject="Religion", skill="religion-image-likeness", priority=2, prompt="According to the current Religion lesson, which pair correctly connects a lesson idea with its supporting detail?", answer="God's image and likeness → people can think, choose, and love", hint="Choose the pair whose two parts are directly connected in the current lesson.", explanation="The current lesson specifically links God's image and likeness with the abilities to think, choose, and love.", choices={"God's image and likeness → people can think, choose, and love","Trinity → people use five senses to enjoy creation","Disciples → three Persons in one God"}},
    {id="auto-religion-creation-care-take-care-of-them-9ee8f3", subject="Religion", skill="religion-creation-care", priority=2, prompt="According to the current Religion lesson, what responsibility is listed for God's gifts of creation?", answer="Take care of them.", hint="Use the responsibility stated directly in the current lesson.", explanation="The current lesson says it is our responsibility to take care of God's gifts of creation.", choices={"Ignore them.","Take care of them.","Use them without responsibility."}},
    {id="auto-religion-creation-care-it-protects-a-resource-instead-of-wasting-it-3afb17", subject="Religion", skill="religion-creation-care", priority=2, prompt="Which explanation best connects turning off running water with the current Religion lesson about caring for God's creation?", answer="It protects a resource instead of wasting it.", hint="Choose the explanation that protects rather than wastes a gift of creation.", explanation="Protecting resources is a concrete way to apply the lesson's responsibility to care for creation.", choices={"It shows that water should never be used.","It changes the number of Persons in the Trinity.","It protects a resource instead of wasting it."}},
    {id="auto-religion-jesus-savior-jesus-is-our-savior-69df60", subject="Religion", skill="religion-jesus-savior", priority=2, prompt="According to the current Religion lesson, which lesson idea is described by “Jesus died for our sins and gives us new life in grace”?", answer="Jesus is our Savior.", hint="Match the description about sin and grace to the lesson title it explains.", explanation="The current lesson connects Jesus as Savior with his death for our sins and the gift of new life in grace.", choices={"Jesus is our Savior.","We use our senses to enjoy creation.","Mary is the mother of the Church."}},
    {id="auto-religion-jesus-savior-he-died-for-our-sins-and-gives-us-new-life-in-grace-89c106", subject="Religion", skill="religion-jesus-savior", priority=2, prompt="According to the current Religion lesson, which two ideas are connected in the explanation of Jesus as our Savior?", answer="He died for our sins and gives us new life in grace.", hint="Choose the pair that the current lesson joins in its explanation of Savior.", explanation="The lesson directly connects salvation from sin with the gift of new life in grace.", choices={"He is Mary’s son and people have five senses.","He died for our sins and gives us new life in grace.","Creation has gifts and disciples are friends."}},
    {id="auto-religion-original-sin-their-sin-is-connected-with-the-human-tendency-to-sin-called-original-sin-2fbde6", subject="Religion", skill="religion-original-sin", priority=2, prompt="According to the current Religion lesson, which statement correctly connects Adam and Eve with original sin?", answer="Their sin is connected with the human tendency to sin called original sin.", hint="Match Adam and Eve with the lesson’s explanation of original sin.", explanation="The current lesson connects Adam and Eve’s sin with the human tendency to sin called original sin.", choices={"They are named as the three Persons of the Trinity.","They gave the Church the sacraments.","Their sin is connected with the human tendency to sin called original sin."}},
    {id="auto-religion-original-sin-the-lesson-connects-their-sin-with-the-human-tendency-to-sin-feef64", subject="Religion", skill="religion-original-sin", priority=2, prompt="According to the current Religion lesson, why are Adam and Eve mentioned when original sin is explained?", answer="The lesson connects their sin with the human tendency to sin.", hint="Choose the explanation that preserves the lesson’s cause-and-result connection.", explanation="Adam and Eve are named because the lesson explains original sin through the connection between their sin and the human tendency to sin.", choices={"The lesson connects their sin with the human tendency to sin.","The lesson says they are sacraments.","The lesson says they are gifts of the Holy Spirit."}},
    {id="auto-religion-faith-gift-helps-us-believe-99afcd", subject="Religion", skill="religion-faith-gift", priority=2, prompt="According to the current Religion lesson, which phrase best completes “Faith is a gift from God that ___”?", answer="helps us believe", hint="Use the exact purpose of faith stated in the lesson.", explanation="The current lesson says faith is a gift from God that helps us believe.", choices={"washes away every mistake we make","helps us believe","names the three Persons of the Trinity"}},
    {id="auto-religion-faith-gift-gift-from-god-helps-us-believe-48165b", subject="Religion", skill="religion-faith-gift", priority=2, prompt="According to the current Religion lesson, which relationship best explains faith?", answer="gift from God → helps us believe", hint="Keep the source and purpose of faith together.", explanation="The lesson directly connects faith with being a gift from God and helping us believe.", choices={"sacred sign → human tendency to sin","Baptism → three Persons in one God","gift from God → helps us believe"}},
